$DIR/main.p:13:14: cannot convert b.String() to an interface: it points into memory allocated from ar, a local arena of f, which is gone before the interface's copy is released
    b.String() points into memory allocated from ar, a local arena of f. ar (a
    local arena of f, declared at main.p:9) was given as an allocator to a call
    that returns a string, or to a value that returns one, and such text may
    have been allocated from the arena. What lives in the arena is gone when the
    arena is. Converting b.String() to an interface puts a copy of it in f's
    frame, and the copy holds a reference to the text until f returns. That is
    after the arena ar is dropped, and releasing the reference would write into
    the arena's memory.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use the text without converting it to an interface, for example by writing
    its bytes:
        os.Stdout.Write(b.Bytes())
    Or convert a string made from an allocator that outlives the arena, passed
    explicitly, or from the default allocator before the arena is installed
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)
